"""devguard - contain misbehaving hardware before it stalls the whole system.

Watches for
  * interrupt storms   (one IRQ line eating a CPU)            -> move the IRQ
                                                                  to a housekeeping CPU
  * PCIe AER floods    (a link spewing correctable errors)     -> turn off ASPM on
                                                                  that link, then mask
                                                                  correctable errors
  * USB reconnect loops (a device/cable dropping off and on)   -> disable that port
  * kernel log floods  (a driver printing hundreds of lines/s) -> lower the console
                                                                  loglevel

Default mode is "observe": it only logs what it would do. Set mode = "enforce"
in /etc/devguard.toml (or pass --enforce) to let it act. Everything it changes
is recorded in /run/devguard/state.json and can be undone with
`devguard revert`.

Usage:
  devguard run [--enforce|--observe] [--config FILE]   run the service
  devguard status                                      show recorded actions
  devguard revert                                      undo recorded actions
  devguard scan [--seconds N]                          observe for N seconds,
                                                       print findings, exit
"""

